import java.io.ObjectInputStream;
import java.util.concurrent.locks.Lock;
import java.util.concurrent.locks.ReentrantLock;

public class StateLoader {

    private static final Lock lock = new ReentrantLock();
    private static MoSTState cachedState = null;

    public static MoSTState loadState(String filePath) {
        try (ObjectInputStream ois = new ObjectInputStream(new FileInputStream(filePath))) {
            lock.lock();
            try {
                if (cachedState == null) {
                    Object obj = ois.readObject();
                    if (obj instanceof MoSTState) {
                        cachedState = (MoSTState) obj;
                        System.out.println("State loaded successfully.");
                    }
                }
                return cachedState;
            } finally {
                lock.unlock();
            }
        } catch (IOException | ClassNotFoundException e) {
            System.err.println("Failed to load state: " + e.getMessage());
            return null;
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Example usage
        MoSTState state = loadState("path/to/state/file.ser");
        if (state != null) {
            // Use the loaded state object
        }
    }
}
```

This code snippet provides a thread-safe and performance-optimized method for loading a `MoSTState` object from a file. It uses a `ReentrantLock` to ensure that only one thread can execute the state loading at a time, preventing race conditions. The method checks if the state is already cached before attempting to load it from the file, which improves performance when multiple threads request the state concurrently.

The `ObjectInputStream` is closed automatically using a try-with-resources statement, ensuring proper resource management and avoiding potential memory leaks. Exception handling is implemented to catch `IOException` and `ClassNotFoundException`, logging any errors that occur during the state loading process.

This implementation can be easily integrated into an existing system with minimal disruption. It maintains backward compatibility with the current state loading mechanism by providing a new method (`loadState`) for reading the state from a file.']

